<!--
    kuna: an alignment PUSH in the prologue and a POP into another register in
    the epilogue invent an extra argument and a 128-bit return.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 call and prototype model, output-prototype.
    P0 assertion:      option retpushedhalf on|off  (default on).

    Source (gas, x86-64 SysV, bytes verbatim below):

        same_reg_restore:  push %rdx ; mov %rdi,%rax ; add $7,%rax ; pop %rdx ; ret
        cross_reg_restore: push %r8  ; mov %rdi,%rax ; add $7,%rax ; pop %rdx ; ret
        dead_rdx_write:    mov %rdi,%rax ; add $7,%rax ; mov %rsi,%rdx ; ret

    All three leave something in RDX at the RETURN, so return recovery offers the
    SysV RAX:RDX pair in all three. They differ in where the RDX value came from.

    The bug (pass 2, option off): in cross_reg_restore the popped slot holds R8,
    which the function pushed only to move the stack pointer. RDX at the RETURN
    therefore traces back to R8 at entry, and kuna_retinputhalf reads that as
    "an input parameter the function PLACED in the return register" and keeps the
    half. Keeping the half is what gives R8 a reader, and R8 having a reader is
    what makes R8 a parameter, so a one-argument function recovers FIVE
    arguments and a 128-bit return, with the fifth used only as the high half.

    The fix (pass 1, default): a register some stack-adjusting instruction stores
    to memory and no instruction ever writes is stack maintenance, not a value the
    function placed anywhere, so it cannot satisfy the placement test. The
    evidence is gathered during the flow build, because by the time the repair
    runs copy propagation has collapsed the store and the load and the surviving
    RDX = COPY(R8) is identical to a deliberate mov %r8,%rdx.

    The two controls are unmoved in both passes. same_reg_restore pops back into
    the register it pushed, so the pop WRITES it and the register is not
    push-only; dead_rdx_write touches no stack at all, and its RDX really is a
    plain copy of the second argument, which is indistinguishable from a genuine
    struct{long,long} return.
-->
